# expect: a swizzle applies to a variable or a field of one, not to this expression
# The INDEX half of the base rule; tests/reject/swizzle_base_not_place.ty is the
# call half. Measured 2026-09-05: `place_key` (src/tychoc.c@place_key) has no key
# for a non-literal index, so `(vs[i].x, vs[i].x) = (7, 8)` is already accepted
# while `(vs[0].x, vs[0].x) = (7, 8)` is refused by name. Admitting an indexed
# base would carry that hole into `vs[i].(x, x)`, which is the spelling the
# feature exists for. The accepting twin is tests/swizzle.ty.
struct V:
    x: int
    y: int

fn main():
    vs := [V(1, 2)]
    i := 0
    vs[i].(x, y) = vs[i].(y, x)
    println(str(vs[i].x))
